  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Provision of Integrated Protection and Life-Saving Health Services to Migrants and Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs) in Yemen</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The humanitarian situation in Yemen has led to considerable suffering across the country. An estimated 4.3 million internally displaced persons, 300,000 migrants, refugees and asylum seekers and millions of conflict-affected host community members are caught in the midst of an active armed conflict where the most basic resources are not available to ensure safety, dignity or protection. Fighting has exacerbated high-risk coping mechanisms, stalled development gains made in the years preceding and driven the country to the brink of famine. 
 
The International Organization for Migration (IOM) and its partners are providing integrated life-saving health and protection support that prioritizes the most vulnerable migrants in Yemen at disembarkation points, along transit routes and hubs, and in locations where migrants are stranded or trapped as a result of conflict. IOM is also providing the wider humanitarian community in Yemen with greater access to information and strategies on how to better support migrants in this complicated and dynamic operational context. While migrant needs will be prioritized under this action, IOM is provide vulnerable internally displaced persons, host community members, and other vulnerable populations in Yemen with critically needed protection and health support, ensuring equal access for all population groups if/when they are encountered.</narrative>
  </description>
